<div class="wrap">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>Download &amp; install</h1>
    <p class="lead">Ferrite goes on the radio from your browser, through the radio's own bootloader. Here is how
      that works, and how to check that a release really comes from us.</p>
  </header>

  {% if not site.release.released %}
  <div class="callout">
    <p><b>The first public release, {{ site.release.next }}, isn't out yet.</b> There is nothing to download
      today. When it is ready, it will be here and in Ferrite Console, and we'll say so on the
      <a href="{{ site.urls.blog }}/">blog</a> and the <a href="{{ site.discord }}">Discord</a>.</p>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What you need</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A Radtel RT-950 Pro and its programming cable.</li>
        <li>A computer with Chrome or Edge, for <a href="{{ site.urls.console }}">Ferrite Console</a>. Safari and
          Firefox can't reach the cable.</li>
        <li>The original firmware file from Radtel (the <code>.BTF</code>). You need it to go back, and the HF
          receiver's SSB support comes from it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How installing works</h2>
      <p>Everything happens on Console's <b>Install</b> page, over the USB cable.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><b>Connect.</b> Plug in the cable and turn the radio on. Any firmware is fine.</li>
        <li><b>Back up the whole radio.</b> It takes about a minute and a half. Console won't install until the
          backup is done and you have saved the file. Keep it safe, off the computer too.</li>
        <li><b>SSB, optional.</b> Drop in your Radtel <code>.BTF</code>: Console takes the SSB support from it,
          on your computer.</li>
        <li><b>Install.</b> About a minute. The radio restarts, Ferrite sets up its storage, and setup asks a few
          questions: callsign, region, time, look.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Your calibration is never erased. Installing replaces the radio's channels and settings, so you add your
        channels again after; Console can import a CHIRP CSV.</p>

      <h2>Going back to stock</h2>
      <p>Going back is built into the same page. Console checks the backup is this radio's own, writes your old
        channels and settings back, then installs the original firmware. The radio restarts as it was before
        Ferrite.</p>
      <p>The bootloader is the radio's own and is never changed, so an install or an update can always be tried
        again.</p>

      <h2 id="verify">Checking a release</h2>
      <p>Every release image comes with a <a href="https://jedisct1.github.io/minisign/">minisign</a> signature, a
        <code>.minisig</code> file beside it. Ferrite Console checks it before installing. To check one yourself,
        install minisign and run:</p>
    </div>
    <pre class="prose wrapcode"><code>minisign -Vm ferrite-{{ site.release.next }}.btf -P {{ site.minisign.key }}</code></pre>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>It prints "Signature and comment signature verified" for a good file. Anything else, don't install it.</p>
      <p>Ferrite's public key:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="keybox prose"><small>Key ID {{ site.minisign.id }}</small>{{ site.minisign.key }}</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
      <p class="note">Your own builds aren't signed; Console installs them and says so.</p>

      <h2>Updating</h2>
      <p>On a radio already on Ferrite, Console's <b>Firmware Update</b> checks the file, restarts the radio into
        its bootloader and sends the update. Your channels, settings and SSB support stay.</p>
      <p>Step by step, with what to do if the radio doesn't answer: the
        <a href="{{ site.urls.manual }}">user guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
